## 2. Embedding Strategy: **Multi-Level with Hierarchical Indexing**

Do NOT use a flat single-vector index. Design three tiers:

```
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    EMBEDDING ARCHITECTURE                     │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                                              │
│  Tier 1: PAGE METADATA (cheap, always compute)              │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │
│  │ MiniLM-L6v2 embedding of:                               │ │
│  │   title + meta description + first_256_chars            │ │
│  │   + url_path (tokenized) + content_type                 │ │
│  │                                                         │ │
│  │ Dimensions: 384  |  Storage: ~1.5KB/entry               │ │
│  │ Purpose: Fast candidate retrieval (10K → ~100)           │ │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │
│                          ↓ candidates                        │
│  Tier 2: CHUNK DENSE VECTORS (BGE-M3 dense)                │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │
│  │ BGE-M3 1024-dim dense vector per chunk                  │ │
│  │                                                         │ │
│  │ Storage: ~4KB/chunk × avg 8 chunks/doc = ~32KB/page     │ │
│  │ Purpose: Semantic similarity re-ranking                  │ │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │
│                          ↓ top-K                             │
│  Tier 3: SPARSE + COLBERT VECTORS (BGE-M3)                 │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │
│  │ Sparse: ~25K vocab one-hot (stored as indices+values)   │ │
│  │ ColBERT: 128-dim × max_512_tokens per chunk             │ │
│  │                                                         │ │
│  │ Purpose: Exact term matching, fine-grained reranking    │ │
│  │ Storage: ~8KB sparse + ~256KB ColBERT (optional tier)   │ │
│  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │
│                                                              │
│  Query path: Tier1 → Tier2 → Tier3 (cascading refinement)  │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

**Why not just use the embedding from the scrape directly?**

Because Firecrawl's content arrives as **raw markdown** with varying quality. You need to:
1. **Normalize** the content (strip boilerplate, headers, navigation)
2. **Enrich** metadata (auto-detect content type, extract entities)
3. **Structure** for retrieval (not just embedding)

**Implementation in `vector-store.ts`:**

```typescript
// Tier structure per cached URL
interface CachedDocument {
  // Tier 1 - always loaded
  tier1: {
    embedding: Float32Array;    // 384-dim MiniLM
    metadata: {
      url: string;
      title: string;
      contentType: 'article' | 'code' | 'api-doc' | 'discussion';
      scrapedAt: number;
      contentHash: string;      // SHA-256 of normalized content
      firstSeen: number;
      lastAccessed: number;
      accessCount: number;
    };
  };

  // Tier 2 - loaded on candidate match
  tier2: {
    chunks: Array<{
      embedding: Float32Array;  // 1024-dim BGE-M3 dense
      text: string;             // The actual chunk
      startOffset: number;
      tokenCount: number;
    }>;
  };

  // Tier 3 - loaded on high-confidence candidates only
  tier3?: {
    sparseVectors: Array<{
      indices: Uint32Array;
      values: Float32Array;
    }>;
    colbertVectors?: Array<Float32Array>;
  };
}
```

**Storage projection for 10K entries:**

```
Avg page: 5 chunks × 3KB text + 32KB vectors + 8KB sparse = ~55KB
10K pages: 55KB × 10,000 = 550MB total

With SQLite + compression: ~200-300MB on disk
```

This fits comfortably in memory on any Intel machine with 8GB+ RAM.

